#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482647} :activity/id #uuid "65743f6d-c4f5-542f-9721-c15aa32b036f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08db4f16-a7e9-5314-b935-a3eb368dfcef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481788} {:answer/id #uuid "3266316f-36b1-5ff6-a6b0-47a3434780c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481896} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca34fc0-97a3-5fa6-97d2-26038deb9c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481958} {:answer/id #uuid "deb67241-8ca5-5879-a251-c812cf6ac763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482303} {:answer/id #uuid "e80baa43-8f8f-5491-84c0-0c880c87ed98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a676cad1-550b-58ad-bc2d-4c3f63df1e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109579" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6b747f5-b6fd-5eba-ae77-a9174a9eb0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453252} {:answer/id #uuid "52b890bf-1e84-54cf-9889-91651247a068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453253} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c1092e-91a4-5e66-9c90-37891305f270" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc93c4a8-f3e6-52d8-9ef2-057cd1fd00b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453254} {:answer/id #uuid "9e356dd9-cc07-5fef-886f-0f4115f8b97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4825cadc-9f85-54bd-99c2-cb4b67256536" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454377} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604f4f38-dfc8-500b-b033-c1d586698b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453256} {:answer/id #uuid "00d18245-d9fc-5c4f-8e90-f44ae94f6e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453256} :answer-field/id #uuid "205d0d73-77db-5f0e-9eb3-e7a3ed04c9e4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddb46c6-735a-5bdf-b2a2-b57344c5441a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109434" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-4I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $s,u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $s$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\ns\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $s$ to $0$ in $4$ steps, \nthe vector $s$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25c4edf-bc28-5a6d-bce8-dc691f40f46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461575} {:answer/id #uuid "b25784ef-0104-536a-b0f5-9202aac0e151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461575} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1cae5fa-1b6c-55f0-b295-ad6fed3c38ae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858d8067-b0de-598a-b606-236eb811c558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461577} {:answer/id #uuid "4852a013-cee8-55ea-a31e-65f55bad1029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461578} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ccb795-d8ad-55c8-a867-52adde8a804d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6f3d6d1-2d3e-5256-8645-40f5fb25a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461579} {:answer/id #uuid "ef647bd5-b2da-51b9-8380-c98b65a2a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed24b2ea-7eb1-5ee6-99ae-1a38c15df738" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a65ad08d-f6cf-551f-aa17-5ecb903998d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109524" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08290cf-97b2-57cf-844f-51f3f8679205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461593} {:answer/id #uuid "f4730faa-ba57-5426-abd9-1a399ed14be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461593} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f461851-4c29-56b5-b587-ece99f77d200" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6858fc7c-2f0f-5b3c-ae95-ae88e0a473d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461595} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf1fc22-4ba0-5222-8c5f-7f5ea96efe9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461595} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d42734-c059-5048-8028-b026cadd6c1f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148aa426-7c69-5bf8-b66e-2a9a3a68ebce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461597} {:answer/id #uuid "a11fa5ad-4405-5250-9ebf-b7d7766ffecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5120cbb6-223a-5776-bd9b-e6744de4fa26" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "899bf4b0-0a2e-5149-9510-dff10d732f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-109586" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-8I:$\n\n$$\nA-8I=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-8I:$\n\n$$\n(A-8I)v=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-8I:$\n\n$$\n(A-8I)w=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-8I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488d514c-7856-5bb1-9f27-61a06e12356e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447986} {:answer/id #uuid "2a601700-14b1-5b37-bc21-e9cb82917252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447986} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd010aa9-624f-5401-9ca7-3ba049f13042" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268e1ff9-ffef-534e-9702-84cc79d956ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447988} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ecc2bf-cf19-5dcd-8dc0-e365d5028bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447988} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad696ab-44e1-56ba-b3ee-b3265cbb73ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b058b5-afc0-5cd6-ae4d-bd0a5bc33886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447990} {:answer/id #uuid "69ae4757-e4e5-5d91-83ff-acbbd8852af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447991} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c7aab5-a6e9-5835-b3b5-7835e5599b92" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c683813-90e6-5581-9ed4-32de3ac9d89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109431" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+20I$ and $A-15I$ on the nonzero vectors $v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =15$ of $A$\n- **III.** $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$  of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-15I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+20I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "be1b9ef5-0697-50a6-856c-790402f18621" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482633}}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481770}]}
